R = {}
R["«EXEC»"] = """*(Synthetic ratings with hidden fraud labels, built on the documented design of the BIRDNEST paper (Flipkart-linked authors): per-user star counts and log-bucketed time gaps, a Dirichlet-multinomial model, and an expected-surprise score. Flipkart's data, labels and results are not reproduced; the paper's top-250 precision of 84.4% is quoted only as a reference point. Our score is our own implementation of the documented idea. Written after the analysis.)*

- **The question:** which accounts should reviewers look at first to catch boosting and defaming ratings, how deep should the queue go, and what is it worth?
- **The score works at the top of the list.** The combined Bayesian score (stars plus time) puts **100% fraud in the top 50 and top 250** (precision 0.58 at 1,000; average precision 0.577 against a 0.0135 random baseline; AUC 0.873). This is against perfect hidden labels, so it is not comparable to the paper's 84.4% from human reviewers.
- **Combining the two signals did not win here.** The **time-only score has higher average precision (0.713) than the sum (0.577)**, and taking the larger of the two scores is best (0.719). The star-only score is weak (AP 0.211, AUC 0.417). Paired bootstrap differences are all significant (Holm p = 0.03, the floor with 200 resamples). The reason is that 4/5-star spam looks like a normal customer when 53% of honest ratings are 5 stars.
- **Bayesian shrinkage matters for thin evidence.** A raw skew score fills **769 of its 800 flags with accounts that have 1 to 2 ratings, and none is fraud** (precision 0.000). The Bayesian star score flags none of those accounts. Raw skew has AP 0.011, roughly the random level.
- **Blind spots are large.** In the top 1,600 flags, recall is **100% for defaming** accounts and **85% for burst-only**, but **63% for boosting**, and only **1.4% for skew-only** and **4.7% for stealth** accounts (2 to 4 ratings). Detection power at a 0.1% false-positive rate is under 5% for accounts with 4 ratings or fewer and reaches 49% at 15 ratings and 99% at 30 (boost style). Star skew adds nothing for 4/5-star spam.
- **The cut-off is hard to set.** A parametric-bootstrap null with Benjamini-Hochberg at 5% flags only **116 accounts, all fraud, with 10.8% recall**; at 1% it flags none. The null is built from data that includes fraud and genuine look-alikes, and its p-value floor is coarse, so it is conservative. Flagging the top 1,600 gives precision 0.41 and 952 honest accounts flagged (batch raters are flagged 3.1% of the time, normal users 1.1%).
- **Labels beat the unsupervised score quickly.** A gradient-boosting model with just **500 random labels (10 fraud)** reaches AP 0.649 against 0.576 for BIRDNEST. Adding **seller concentration** lifts it to 0.732 at 500 labels and **0.967 at 40,000**.
- **Money (assumed):** scaled to 1 crore rating accounts, reviewing the **top 0.25% to 0.5% pays**: base net **₹2.6 crore (top 0.25%) to ₹4.0 crore (top 0.5%) a year**, worst case negative (₹-1.1 to -2.8 crore), best case ₹20.6 to 35.9 crore. Going to 1% or deeper turns the base case negative. Break-even precision is 0.86; only the top 250 per 80,000 users clear it.
- **Decision:** run BIRDNEST-style scoring as a ranked queue, review roughly the top 0.3% to 0.5%, keep random labels for a supervised model, and add seller-level features."""
R["«O5»"] = "Cleaning removed 1,500 duplicate rows, 60 invalid star values and 80 missing timestamps, leaving 269,310 ratings. 27.8% of users have a single rating and 39.6% have 2 to 3, so most accounts carry thin evidence (the documented data has about 3 ratings per user). The hidden truth has 1,076 fraud accounts (1.34%) who wrote 13,653 fake ratings (5.07% of all ratings)."
R["«O61»"] = "Fraud accounts rate in bursts: 53.3% of their gaps are under 10 minutes against 10.6% for honest accounts. Their mean stars are 3.91 against 4.10 for honest accounts, so the average hides the pattern (boosters give 4 to 5, defamers give 1 to 2). The honest group has two timing modes (same-sitting and weeks apart) and 6,339 genuine look-alikes (1,540 enthusiasts, 4,799 batch raters) who will produce false alarms."
R["«O71»"] = "Users really do differ: the Dirichlet-multinomial beats a plain multinomial on stars by a likelihood-ratio statistic of 13,907 (p < 1e-300) and on time gaps by a log-likelihood gain of about 1,640. The global star Dirichlet has concentration alpha0 = 5.63 (mean star shares 7.9, 6.0, 9.6, 23.5, 53.0%), so individual users spread widely around the mean. BIC favours K = 2 for stars (254,441 vs 254,516 for K = 1; K = 3 is 254,472) and K = 3 for time (400,531 vs 400,745 for K = 2 and 408,530 for K = 1). The mixtures help the fit most for time, where the gain from K = 1 to K = 2 is about 3,999 log-likelihood. We keep K = 1 for scoring, as the documented 'global fitted distribution', and note this as a simplification."
R["«O72»"] = "At the top of the list the combined score is perfect (P@50 = P@250 = 1.00, P@1000 = 0.58, AP 0.577, AUC 0.873). But it is not the best score. NEST time only has AP 0.713 and AUC 0.939, and NEST max has AP 0.719 and AUC 0.941. NEST stars only is weak (AP 0.211, AUC 0.417, below chance on AUC because 4/5-star spam looks normal and the star signal mainly finds defamers). The raw skew score has AP 0.011, close to the 0.0135 random level, and raw burst share has P@250 of only 0.028. Plain ratings volume has AUC 0.938 but AP 0.232: heavy raters are a useful flag in this simulation because we gave fraud accounts many ratings, which flatters it. H1 is rejected, but in the opposite direction from the one expected: the sum does not beat the best single-signal score."
R["«O73»"] = "The scatter shows fraud accounts in the high-timing-surprise region and a band of defamers at high star surprise, while boosters sit among honest points on the star axis. The top-250 list consists of 167 defaming, 44 burst-only and 39 boosting accounts and no honest accounts. The honest and fraud score distributions overlap heavily in the middle, which is why precision falls to 0.58 by rank 1,000."
R["«O74»"] = "All six paired comparisons are significant with Holm correction (p_holm = 0.03; the bootstrap p-value cannot go below 1/201 with 200 resamples). The combined score beats NEST stars only (ΔAP +0.366, CI 0.342 to 0.391), raw skew (+0.565, CI 0.538 to 0.591), raw burst share (+0.532) and volume (+0.344). It loses to NEST time only (ΔAP -0.136, CI -0.154 to -0.118) and to NEST max (-0.142, CI -0.161 to -0.126). The finding is that adding a star z-score that is uninformative for boosters to a strong time score dilutes it; a max or weighted combination would be better."
R["«O75»"] = "Shrinkage is the clearest win. Flagging the top 800, raw skew picks 769 accounts with 1 to 2 ratings (668 with one rating) and none of them is fraud (precision 0.000 in every bucket up to 5 ratings and zero overall); the Bayesian star score picks none of the 1-2 rating accounts (Fisher p < 1e-300). Overall precision of the top 800 is 0.000 for raw skew, 0.279 for NEST stars and 0.681 for BIRDNEST. By evidence level, BIRDNEST precision is 0.505 at 6 to 9 ratings and 0.934 at 10 or more (485 flags). Accounts with 10 or more ratings are 21% fraud (738 of 3,476), so the model's precision is largely a function of how many ratings an account has left. H2 is rejected."
R["«O76»"] = "Recall in the top 1,600 differs sharply by type (chi-square 525, p < 1e-100). Defaming is caught completely (216 of 216, 100%). Burst-only is 85.2% and boosting is 63.5% (CI 59.0% to 67.8%); the time-only score gets 88.1% of boosters, so the star component is not helping them. Skew-only (1.4%) and stealth (4.7%) accounts are almost invisible: skew-only accounts show no burst and a 4/5-star pattern that looks normal, and stealth accounts leave only 2 to 4 ratings. Precision in this list is 0.405 and recall 0.602, with 952 honest accounts flagged (791 normal users, 150 batch raters, 11 enthusiasts). Batch raters are flagged 3.1% of the time against 1.1% for normal users. H3 is rejected."
R["«O79»"] = "The stratified null gives well-behaved p-values for ordinary honest users (median p of 'normal' users 0.547), yet BH at a nominal 5% flags just 116 accounts, all of them fraud (realised false-discovery share 0.000, 95% CI 0 to 0.032), with recall of 10.8%. At 1% and under Bonferroni nothing is flagged. The test is conservative: the global fit absorbs fraud and genuine look-alikes, and with 8 copies of the user base the smallest possible p-value is coarse for the larger strata. H4 is not rejected, but the rule leaves almost 90% of fraud unflagged, so it is a safe auto-action list rather than a review queue. A review queue should use rank depth instead."
R["«O710»"] = "Power rises steeply with evidence. For a booster-style account at a 0.1% false-positive rate, detection is 2.9% with 2 ratings, 3.6% with 4, 19.8% with 10, 48.9% with 15, 73.9% with 20 and 99.2% with 30. Burst-only accounts are similar (4.0%, 5.0%, 26.3%, 53.9%, 74.4%, 98.7%). Skew-only accounts are never detected (0% at every size up to 30 ratings), because 80% 5-star ratings is within the normal spread of honest users when the global distribution has alpha0 = 5.63. H5 is not rejected: power depends strongly on the number of ratings. To estimate precision of the top 250 within ±5 points, planning with the paper's documented 84.4%, about 113 expert labels are needed (finite population of 250). Estimating prevalence within ±0.3 points needs about 5,664 random labels. If experts mislabel 5% of cases, measured precision at our perfect 1.00 would read 0.95."
R["«O713»"] = "The most suspicious account has 43 ratings (33 one-star, 10 two-star); its posterior long-run average is 1.57 (95% interval 1.31 to 1.92), a defaming profile with a score of 56.6. An honest account with a single 5-star rating has a raw average of 5.0 but a posterior mean of 4.22 with a wide interval (3.17 to 4.83) and a score of 14.3, close to a typical honest account with 6 ratings (score 14.1, posterior mean 4.12, interval 3.39 to 4.65). This is the shrinkage: one rating barely moves the account away from the global pattern."
R["«O81»"] = "The supervised model overtakes the unsupervised score with very few labels. With 500 random labels (only 10 fraud accounts) gradient boosting on star and timing features has test AP 0.649 (CI 0.603 to 0.689) against 0.576 (CI 0.528 to 0.614) for BIRDNEST, and 0.875 at 40,000 labels (537 fraud). Adding seller concentration helps a great deal: 0.732 at 500 labels, 0.802 at 2,000, 0.925 at 8,000 and 0.967 at 40,000 (CI 0.957 to 0.976). In this simulation a strong extra signal (a boosting account concentrates on one seller) matters more than the choice of unsupervised or supervised method. The caveat is that our fraud is easy to describe and the labels are perfect and random; real expert labels come mostly from the top of a queue and are noisy."
R["«O9»"] = "The rating distortion is concentrated in defamed sellers: the mean shift is -0.18 stars for 345 defamed sellers and only +0.05 for 801 boosted sellers (mean absolute shift 0.09 across 1,148 targeted sellers), because boosters add 4/5 stars to a base that is already 4.1 on average. Reviewing the top 400 accounts (0.5% of users) removes 59.8% of the distortion with precision 0.97. On our assumptions the base case nets about ₹4.0 crore a year at that depth (benefit ₹6.3 crore, cost ₹2.3 crore) and ₹2.6 crore at the top 200. The worst case is negative at every depth (₹-1.1 crore at 200 and ₹-2.8 crore at 400), the best case is ₹20.6 crore at 200 and ₹35.9 crore at 400. At 800 accounts (1%) precision falls to 0.68 and the base case becomes ₹-4.1 crore. These are assumptions-driven; only the rating shift and the removal share are measured."
R["«O91»"] = "In the base case, one removed fraud account is worth about ₹780 a year, so the break-even precision is 0.86. Only the first band (ranks 1 to 250, precision 1.00) clears it; the second (251 to 500) has precision 0.796 and falls just short, and later bands fall from 0.264 (501 to 1,000) to 0.006 (8,001 to 16,000). Clearing every true fraud account in the simulation would take 11 analyst-days at 100 accounts a day. The economics therefore support a short, high-precision queue, and the cut-off is set by precision per band, not by an overall flag rate."
R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation**

1. **Run the BIRDNEST-style score as a ranked review queue**, not as an automatic ban list. Review the top 250 to 400 accounts per 80,000 rating users (about 0.3% to 0.5%); precision is 1.00 for the top 250 and 0.97 at 400 on our simulated data.
2. **Do not rely on the sum of the star and time scores.** Use the time score or the maximum of the two, and add seller concentration (share of an account's ratings on one seller), which lifted supervised AP from 0.649 to 0.732 with only 500 labels.
3. **Rely on shrinkage, not raw skew.** Raw skew filled the flags with single-rating accounts and caught no fraud.
4. **Collect random labels every cycle** (about 5,700 for a prevalence estimate within ±0.3 points, about 113 for precision of the top 250 within ±5 points) and move to a supervised model once labels exist.
5. **Accept the blind spots:** skew-only drip fraud (1.4% recall) and 2 to 4 rating stealth accounts (4.7%) need seller, device or text signals.

**Decision rule:** keep reviewing down the list while the precision of a rank band is above the break-even p* = (c + f) / (V + f), 0.86 in the base case. Use the BH-controlled list (116 accounts at 5% on our data) for automatic action only if a second signal agrees. Re-check the cut-off every cycle.

**KPIs:** precision of the top-250 band (reviewed), share of reviewed accounts that are batch raters or enthusiasts (false alarms), rating shift removed for targeted sellers, recall on the random label sample, review cost per confirmed account.

**Not yet known:** real fraud prevalence, how real fraudsters adapt after bans, noisy-expert-label effects, and any rupee impact (the paper reports none)."""
